{
  "result": "That's a big one to land on, but if I had to pick just one change, it would be this:\n\nOnce in their life, everyone would experience a full, vivid day as someone completely different from themselves \u2014 different age, income, country, body, background, beliefs \u2014 and they'd keep the memory of it when they woke up back in their own life.\n\nNot just hearing their story. Actually living in it for 24 hours. The exhaustion, the worries, the small joys, the way the world reacts to you.\n\nI think most of the worst things in the world don't come from people being cartoon villains. They come from being absolutely certain we understand other people, without ever having to *be* them, even for a day. It's easy to make policy, or judgments, or just casual decisions, when the consequences for other people feel abstract.\n\nIf that abstraction disappeared \u2014 if you *knew* in your gut what it's like to be the person on the other side of your decision \u2014 war, cruelty, neglect, and a lot of our political stubbornness would become much harder to justify.\n\nIt wouldn't solve everything, of course. Disease and climate change would still need science and work. But I suspect we'd make very different choices about them, because \"future people\" and \"people far away\" wouldn't feel far away anymore.\n\nWhat about you? If you got one world-edit, what would you use it on?",
